L172: 1
L173: 2
L174: **BACKGROUND**
L175: Pregnancy is often regarded as a period of high risk when it comes to development of e.g.
L176: excessive weight gain, gestational diabetes and musculo-skeletal problems such as low back
L177: and pelvic girdle pain and urinary and fecal incontinence (Romem et al. 1991, Haugen 1998,
L178: Warren et al. 1997, Mørkved 2003).
L179: While a pregnancy implies these and other risks, exercise is regarded as advantageous during
L180: the period, and is recommended in order to reduce negative symptoms, both physical and
L181: psychological (SEF 2000, ACOG 2003). Exercise is defined as regular, leisure-time physical
L182: activities, aimed improving physical condition, ability or health. Thus exercise must have a
L183: high enough level of intensity and regularity to improve the function of the heart and
L184: respiratory systems and muscles (Bouchard et al. 1993). Most published studies indicate that
L185: women with normal, uncomplicated pregnancies are fit for exercise with few restrictions,
L186: without the risk of hurting either themselves or their child (Sternfeld 1997, Riemann &
L187: Hansen 2000). In a review, Clapp (1996) concludes that regular exercise (30 min up to 5 days
L188: a week) is not negative for the fetus and has several positive effects on the mother.
L189: Various epidemiological studies have examined the occurrence of different pregnancy-related
L190: diseases, and the relationship between such diseases and physical activity among pregnant
L191: women. While the pregnancy is a high-risk period for becoming overweight, the condition of
L192: being overweight during pregnancy is a risk factor for both the mother and the fetus (Baeten
L193: et al. 2001). Overweight in pregnancy is associated with fetal macrosomatia, and the
L194: combination is associated with prolonged labour, low apgar score, shoulder dystocia, nerve
L195: plexus injuries, increased proportion of instrumental deliveries and perineal ruptures Øian
L196: (2000). A greater increase in weight than 15 kilos implies a higher risk of developing disease
L197: in pregnancy and complications during labour (Haram et al. 1997), as well as problems with
L198: weight reduction after the birth (Rooney & Schauberger 2002). Some studies reveal that
L199: women who exercise before and during pregnancy weigh less, and are less likely to gain too
L200: much weight than those who do not (Clapp & Little 1995, Clapp 2000), while other studies
L201: conclude that exercise does not affect the weight of the mother. (Lokey et al. 1991, Sternfeld
L202: et al. 1995, Marquez-Sterling et al. 2000). These conflicting results are probably due to the
L203: difficulty of assessing the effect of exercise only, since weight gain is also dependent on food
L204: intake and other factors (Rössner 2000).
L205: Gestational diabetes may be related to being overweight, and the prevalence varies between
L206: 5% and 12% (Baeten et al. 2001). There has been surprisingly little focus in scientific
L207: literature on the effects of physical activity during pregnancy in order to prevent gestational
L208: diabetes, despite the fact that the prevalence increases. The training of large muscle groups
L209: during pregnancy seems to have a positive effect on pregnancy-related diabetes, due to better
L210: insulin sensitivity and utilisation of glucose, and furthermore to a normalised level of blood
L211: sugar (Jovanovic-Peterson et al.1989, Garcia-Patterson et al. 2001). Dye et al. (1997) found
L212: that the effects of exercise might be positive when it comes to primary prevention of
L213: pregnancy-related diabetes. Exercise reduced the women’s risk for developing pregnancyrelated diabetes by 47 % among women with BMI>33.
L214: Almost the half of all Norwegian women (42.4%) state that they have been troubled by low
L215: back / pelvic girdle pain during pregnancy (Endresen 1995). For many women, the pain
L216: 2
L217: 3
L218: causes difficulties in their everyday work and life; for some of them, the experience from their
L219: pregnancy and postpartum period is dominated by pain, disability and reduced quality of life.
L220: The extent of these problems makes it a considerable problem not only for the women but
L221: also for society. Low back and pelvic girdle pain is the most common reason for sick leave for
L222: pregnant Swedish women (Østgaard et al. 1994a), and it has been reported that pregnancyrelated pelvic girdle pain is the reason for approximately 300 000 days of sick leave per year
L223: in Denmark (Larsen et al. 1999).
L224: Many women have continued pain in the lower back and pelvic girdle during the postpartum
L225: period (Mørkved et al. 1998, Larsen et al.1999), and about 1/5 of the women get chronic pain
L226: (Larsen et al. 1999, Østgaard et al. l997). There are several indications that the normal
L227: physiological changes during pregnancy lead to mechanical and structural changes which
L228: might cause low back and pelvic girdle pain during pregnancy and after delivery (Vleeming et
L229: al 1997). Most hypotheses about the cause of low back and pelvic girdle pain focus on
L230: changes in posture and reduced stability resulting from weight gain and hormonal influences.
L231: Several studies show changes in motor control strategies in women with pelvic girdle pain
L232: (Avery et al. 2000, O'Sullivan et al. 2002, Hungerford et al. 2003). Results from other studies
L233: indicate that exercise does have an effect on low back pain during pregnancy (Norén et al.
L234: 1997, Kihlstrand et al. 1999) and on pelvic girdle pain after delivery (Stuge et al. 2004).
L235: However, there is still uncertainty on the question of whether or not exercise during
L236: pregnancy can prevent pelvic girdle pain.
L237: Pregnancy and childbirth are regarded as important risk periods in the development of
L238: incontinence. Prevalence of urinary incontinence varies between studies, with average
L239: prevalence estimates of 30-40% during pregnancy and the first months after childbirth
L240: (Mørkved & Bø 1999, Hunskaar et al. 2002, Mørkved 2003). Mørkved et al. (2003) found in a
L241: randomised clinical study that specific exercise of the pelvic floor muscles during pregnancy
L242: had effect on the prevention and treatment of urinary incontinence. A similar effect of pelvic
L243: floor muscle exercise has been reported in other studies, both during pregnancy (Reilly et al.
L244: 2002) and after childbirth (Mørkved & Bø 1997, Mørkved & Bø 2000). Prevalence of fecal
L245: incontinence after delivery is 4-5% (Mørkved & Bø 1999), and no RCT evaluating the effect
L246: of pelvic floor muscle exercise to prevent and treat fecal incontinence in a general pregnant
L247: and postpartum population has been published.
L248: There is a myth that women who exercise regularly have stronger pelvic floor muscles that
L249: may obstruct labour (Riemann et al 2000), but there is no documentation for this claim
L250: (Lokey et al. 1991, Sternfeld et al. 1995, Horns et al. 1996). However, results from
L251: observational studies indicate that pregnant women who exercise regularly all through their
L252: pregnancy have more normal vaginal deliveries (Clapp 1990, Bungum et al. 2000, Clapp
L253: 2000), shorter active expulsion time and fewer complications during labour than those who do
L254: not exercise (Clapp 1990). In a randomised clinical trial, Salvesen and Mørkved (2004) found
L255: that fewer women who followed a scheme for specific strength exercise for the pelvic floor
L256: muscles during pregnancy had a prolonged second stage of labour, compared to a control
L257: group. Moreover, there was a lower prevalence of episiotomy and fewer cases of breech births
L258: in the exercise group (Salvesen & Mørkved 2004). This study is currently the only published
L259: randomised clinical trial which has tested such consequences of exercise before labour.
L260: Today’s knowledge about the importance of exercise during pregnancy is mainly based on
L261: observational data from epidemiological studies, and the scientific strength of the clinical
L262: 3
L263: 4
L264: recommendations given is open to question. There is a great lack of results from randomised
L265: clinical trials with high methodological quality, assessing the effects of exercise during
L266: pregnancy. As a result of this, many important questions are still not answered. One of these
L267: is the effect exercise during pregnancy has in the prevention and treatment of disease and
L268: complications which may arise during pregnancy. Another question is the consequences that
L269: exercise during pregnancy has for labour and delivery.
L270: **This study is thus designed to find answers to the following:**
L271: - **Does regular exercise during pregnancy aid in preventing gestational diabetes?**
L272: - **Does regular exercise during pregnancy prevent low back and/or pelvic girdle pain?**
L273: - **Does regular exercise during pregnancy prevent urine and/or fecal incontinence?**
L274: - **Does regular exercise during pregnancy have an effect on labour and delivery?**
L275: - **Does regular exercise during pregnancy prevent maternal excessive weight gain and**
L276: **fetal macrosomatia?**
L277: **MATERIAL AND METHODS**
L278: **Subjects**
L279: Pregnant women who attend the routine ultrasound control at the three hospitals at 18 weeks
L280: of pregnancy are invited to participate in the study. Women are eligible for the trial if they are
L281: 18 years or more, with a singleton live foetus at the routine ultrasound scan. Exclusion criteria
L282: are pregnancy complications, high risk for preterm labour, pain during pelvic floor muscle
L283: contractions, ongoing urinary tract infection, or diseases that could interfere with participation
L284: (following recommendations from SEF 2000, ACOG 2003). In addition, women who live too
L285: far from the hospitals to be able to attend weekly exercise groups will be excluded. A total of
L286: 800 women will be included. A 12 months inclusion period is planned, in accordance with
L287: experiences from previous studies where 20% of eligible pregnant women agreed to
L288: participate in the trial (Mørkved & Bø 1997, Mørkved et al 2003).
L289: The procedures to be followed will be in accordance with the ethical standards of the
L290: responsible regional committee on human experimentation and with the Helsinki declaration.
L291: **Design**
L292: We are planning a randomised clinical trial (RCT) with two arms; one group will attend a
L293: standardised regular exercise course for 12 weeks during pregnancy (Mørkved et al. 2003)
L294: (exercise group); another group will follow the standard procedure (control group). The trial
L295: is a multi-center study with blocked design, involving three hospitals in Trøndelag and Møre
L296: and Romsdal where a total of approximately 5000 women give birth annually (St.Olavs
L297: Hospital, Levanger Hospital and Ålesund Hospital). Participants at each hospital will be
L298: randomised to an exercise group or a control group. All participants will receive information
L299: concerning nutrition. Measurements will be taken of all participants before and after the
L300: intervention period during pregnancy, and 8 weeks and 1 year after delivery.
L301: _Randomisation procedure_
L302: 4
L303: 5
L304: A computerised randomisation procedure will be used.
L305: **Power calculation**
L306: The power calculations will be made taking into account the diseases we want to treat/prevent
L307: with the lowest prevalence (faecal incontinence and gestational diabetes (Mørkved & Bø
L308: 1999, Baeten et al 2001). We aim at reducing the prevalence from 5% to 1% in the exercise
L309: group. Based on these assumptions a two way t-test with a 5% level of significance and test
L310: strength of 0.90 give a study population of approximately 400 patients in each group (Altman
L311: 1991).
L312: **Evaluation methods**
L313: According to the aims of study, we have several primary outcome measures.
L314: Several variables are routinely registered during pregnancy and labour:
L315: _During pregnancy_
L316:  - Weight, height (BMI)
L317: _Labour and delivery_
L318: - Mode of delivery
L319: - Epidural analgesia or oxytoxin augmentation during labour
L320: - Episiotomy
L321: - Perineal tears
L322: - Neonatal outcomes
L323: - Duration of 1. and 2. stages of labour
L324: Other variables are registered by validated clinical measurement tools and/or questionnaires
L325: for registration of the women’s own experiences:
L326: - _**Gestational diabetes:**_
L327: Oral glucose tolerance test at around 26 weeks
L328: - _**Low back and pelvic girdle pain**_
L329: Pain intensity 100mm Visual Analogue Scale
L330: Disability Rating Index (Salèn et al. 1994)
L331: Registration of sick leaves
L332: Clinical tests
L333: Active straight leg raise (ASLR) (Mens et al. 2000)
L334: Posterior pain provocation test (P4) (Kristiansson et al. 1996, Østgaard et al.
L335: 1994b)
L336: Balance (variations)
L337: - _**Incontinence (urinary/faecal)**_
L338: Self-reports of urinary/faecal incontinence. (Women reporting urinary/faecal
L339: incontinence once per week or more during the previous month are categorised as
L340: incontinent)
L341: Change in continence status (better, unchanged, worse)
L342: _**Strength and function of the pelvic floor muscles**_
L343: Vaginal palpation and observation during contraction used to assess the women's
L344: ability to perform pelvic floor muscle contraction. Pelvic floor muscle strength
L345: (vaginal squeeze pressure, cm H2O) measured by a vaginal balloon catheter (balloon
L346: size 6.7 x 1.7 cm) connected to a pressure transducer (Camtech Ltd. 1300 Sandvika,
L347: 5
L348: 6
L349: Norway). The method was found to be reliable and valid in a previous study (Bø et al
L350: 1990a).
L351: - _**Registration of nutrition**_
L352: Questionnaire (Nord-kost) (Nes et al. 1992).
L353: **Statistical analysis**
L354: The principal analysis will be done on an intention-to-treat basis. The missing last values are
L355: carried forward by their baseline values. Groups are compared with exact computation of
L356: Pearson χ [2 ] - test if data are categorical. Relative risks and their 95% confidence intervals are
L357: calculated for comparisons of proportions [observed ratio of proportions (StatXact -5)].
L358: Normality will be evaluated by using the Shapiro-Wilk W test for normality, and the MannWhitney U test will be used to compare distributions between groups when variables are not
L359: normally distributed (SPSS-10). The influence of covariates on the primary outcome variables
L360: will be explored using logistic regression for odds ratio (SPSS-10). Additional subgroup
L361: analyses will be carried out. Results will be given as mean values with 95% confidence
L362: intervals (CI). P-values < 0.05 will be considered significant.
L363: **Interventions**
L364: Before randomisation, a physiotherapist will provide all women with information related to
L365: food intake and with individual instruction in pelvic floor anatomy and how to contract the
L366: pelvic floor muscles correctly (Bø et al 1990a).
L367: The exercises group will follow a specially designed exercise course including specific
L368: exercises for stabilisation of the lower back and pelvis, the pelvic floor muscles, and general
L369: exercises including balance exercises. They will exercise with a physiotherapist for 60
L370: minutes once a week for a period of 12 weeks (between 20 and 36 pregnancy weeks). In
L371: addition, the women will be encouraged to follow a home exercise program including 45
L372: minutes of exercise twice a week (30 minutes endurance exercise and 15 minutes
L373: strength/balance exercise) and daily pelvic floor muscle contractions. Motivation will be
L374: strongly emphasised by the physiotherapists. Adherence to the training protocol will be based
L375: on registrations in the women's personal training diary and the reports from the
L376: physiotherapists who lead the group exercises. The training protocol follows
L377: recommendations from SEF (2000) and ACOG (2003).
L378: Women in the control group will receive the customary information provided by their
L379: midwife or general practitioner. They will not be discouraged from exercising on their own.
